  <content>
  <section><title>Activity (15 minutes) </title>
<para>The goal of this activity is to introduce the data students will examine in the lesson. They will eventually try to model these city populations with an appropriate linear or exponential function, but the starting point for any model is to ask questions about and observe general trends in the data.</para>
<title>Student Activity </title>
<blockquote>The table below shows the populations of three cities during different years. Take a moment to think of at least one thing you notice and one thing you wonder.</blockquote>
<table class="os-raise-wideequaltable">
  <thead>
   
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
        City
      </entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
        1950
      </entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
        1960
      </entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
        1970
      </entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
        1980
      </entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
        1990
      </entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
        2000
      </entry>
    </row> </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        Paris
      </entry>
      <entry>
        6,300,000
      </entry>
      <entry>
        7,400,000
      </entry>
      <entry>
        8,200,000
      </entry>
      <entry>
        8,700,000
      </entry>
      <entry>
        9,300,000
      </entry>
      <entry>
        9,700,000
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
